+### [Wavelet-to-Gaussian Transition Bank](17351-wavelet-to-gaussian-transition-bank.md)
+The Wavelet-to-Gaussian Transition Bank is a method for constructing Gaussian surrogates for wavelet kernels by fitting a single canonical Gaussian per subband and obtaining others via spatial translation, avoiding redundant fitting for every wavelet kernel. It solves the computational bottleneck of independently fitting Gaussians to every wavelet coefficient in a multi-scale decomposition, enabling efficient analytical conversion from wavelet bases to Gaussian primitives for real-time volumetric visualization. It differs from sibling methods like [GSGEN](8985-gsgen.md) or [Feed-forward and Generalizable Prediction](15832-feed-forward-and-generalizable-prediction.md) by relying on precomputed analytical mappings derived from the translation properties of the discrete wavelet transform (DWT) rather than learned neural networks or random sampling.
+
 ### GaMeS Parametrization
-GaMeS Parametrization parameterizes Gaussian components into flat triangle-face meshes, allowing GS to be visualized and manipulated as a triangle soup [S3, unofficial]. For mesh-bound Gaussians, means are assigned to mesh faces via learnable barycentric coordinates of vertices, ensuring they remain bound under deformation [S3, unofficial]. The orientation of mesh-bound Gaussians is defined by the face normal and two in-plane directions derived from the vertices [S3, unofficial]. The forward mapping $T(\cdot)$ defines triangle vertices $v_1 = m$, $v_2 = m + s_2 r_2$, and $v_3 = m + s_3 r_3$ from a Gaussian $N(m, R, S)$ with $R=[r_1, r_2, r_3]$ and $S=\text{diag}(\epsilon, s_2, s_3)$ [S3, unofficial]. The inverse mapping $T^{-1}(\cdot)$ recovers the Gaussian from a triangle $V=[v_1, v_2, v_3]$ using formulas for mean, rotation, and scaling, where the covariance matrix is recovered as $\hat{\Sigma} = \hat{R}\hat{S}\hat{S}^T\hat{R}^T$ [S3, unofficial].
+GaMeS Parametrization is a parameterization of Gaussian components into flat triangle-face meshes, allowing GS to be visualized and manipulated as a triangle soup [S3, unofficial]. For mesh-bound Gaussians, means are assigned to mesh faces via learnable barycentric coordinates of vertices, ensuring they remain bound under deformation [S3, unofficial]. The orientation of mesh-bound Gaussians is defined by the face normal and two in-plane directions derived from the vertices [S3, unofficial]. The forward mapping $T(\cdot)$ from Gaussian $N(m, R, S)$ with $R=[r_1, r_2, r_3]$ and $S=\text{diag}(\epsilon, s_2, s_3)$ defines triangle vertices as $v_1 = m$, $v_2 = m + s_2 r_2$, and $v_3 = m + s_3 r_3$ [S3, unofficial]. The inverse mapping $T^{-1}(\cdot)$ recovers the Gaussian from triangle $V=[v_1, v_2, v_3]$ using:
+$$ \hat{m} = v_1, \quad \hat{r}_1 = \frac{v_2 - v_1}{\|v_2 - v_1\|}, \quad \hat{r}_2 = \frac{(v_2 - v_1) \times (v_3 - v_1)}{\|(v_2 - v_1) \times (v_3 - v_1)\|}, \quad \hat{r}_3 = \text{orth}(v_3 - v_1; \hat{r}_1, \hat{r}_2) $$
+$$ \hat{s}_1 = \epsilon, \quad \hat{s}_2 = \|v_2 - v_1\|, \quad \hat{s}_3 = \langle v_3 - v_1, \hat{r}_3 \rangle $$
+The covariance matrix is recovered as $\hat{\Sigma} = \hat{R}\hat{S}\hat{S}^T\hat{R}^T$ [S3, unofficial].
+
+### Gamba
