 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** Feed-forward inference is significantly faster than per-scene optimization methods, as it avoids iterative gradient descent. The complexity is dominated by the sparse 3D CNN feature extraction and the MLP head predictions for each point in the global point cloud [S2].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** Requires storage for the global point cloud $P$, the feature volume $F$, and the MLP parameters. The sparse 3D CNN helps manage memory compared to dense volumes [S2].
-*   **Measured Performance:** EVolSplat achieves real-time rendering speeds for urban scenes [S2]. It improves global consistency and reduces ghosting artifacts compared to pixel-aligned methods like MVSplat, Flash3D, and DrivingForward [S2].
-*   **Strengths:** Efficient feed-forward prediction, global consistency, reduced ghosting artifacts, handles large-scale urban scenes [S2].
-*   **Limitations:** Relies on the quality of monocular depth estimators; noisy depth can lead to inaccurate initial positions, though the recursive offset helps mitigate this [S2].
-*   **When to Use:** Large-scale urban scene reconstruction, autonomous driving applications, scenarios requiring fast feed-forward reconstruction [S2].
-*   **When Not to Use:** Small-scale scenes with high overlap where per-scene optimization is feasible and may yield higher fidelity, or scenes where accurate metric depth is unavailable and cannot be estimated reliably [S2].
+*   **Time Complexity:** The method operates in a feed-forward manner, avoiding the slow, per-scene optimization required by standard 3DGS [S2]. The use of a sparse 3D CNN and downsampling of initial primitives reduces computation compared to pixel-aligned methods [S2].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** The global volumetric representation requires memory for the feature volume $F$ and the point cloud $P$. While it reduces redundancy compared to per-pixel methods, it may have a larger footprint than local volume methods due to the global nature [S2].
+*   **Measured Performance:** EVolSplat achieves real-time rendering by feed-forward prediction of Gaussian primitives [S2]. It is designed for urban scene reconstruction from sparse input views [S2].
+*   **Scaling:** The method scales to urban scenes with large camera movements and small parallax, where methods requiring image overlaps struggle [S2].
+*   **Strengths:** Improves global consistency, reduces ghosting artifacts, handles foreground/background separation, and enables fast feed-forward reconstruction [S2].
+*   **Limitations:** Relies on the quality of off-the-shelf depth estimators; noisy depth predictions require filtering [S2].
+*   **When to use:** Efficient urban view synthesis from sparse views where real-time rendering and global consistency are critical [S2].
+*   **When not to use:** Small-scale scenes with high overlap where per-scene optimization might yield higher fidelity, or scenes where depth estimation is unreliable [S2].
 
 ## Background
-Gaussian Geometry Parameters Prediction, specifically in the form of EVolSplat, was introduced by Sheng Miao et al. in 2025 [S2]. It addresses the limitations of existing feed-forward 3DGS methods that suffer from multi-view inconsistencies and duplicated content [S2]. The method builds on the foundation of 3D Gaussian Splatting [15] and leverages advances in monocular depth estimation and sparse 3D CNNs [S2]. It is used for efficient urban view synthesis and autonomous driving-related applications [S2].
+Gaussian Geometry Parameters Prediction, specifically in the form of EVolSplat, was introduced by Sheng Miao, Jiaxin Huang, Dongfeng Bai, Xu Yan, Hongyu Zhou, Yue Wang, Bingbing Liu, Andreas Geiger, and Yiyi Liao from Zhejiang University, Huawei Noah’s Ark Lab, and the University of Tübingen/Tübingen AI Center [S2]. It was published in CVPR 2025 [S2]. The method addresses limitations in existing feed-forward 3DGS methods like MVSplat, Flash3D, and DrivingForward, which suffer from multi-view inconsistencies and duplicated content due to local volume accumulation [S2]. It builds on the foundation of 3D Gaussian Splatting [15] and integrates depth priors from metric depth estimators [S2].
 
